===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*skey-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|nyce}}, {{m|enm|nice}}, {{m|enm|nys}}, from {{der|en|fro|nice}}, {{m|fro|niche}}, {{m|fro|nisce||simple, foolish, ignorant}}, from {{der|en|la|nescius||ignorant, not knowing}}; compare {{m|la|nesciō||to know not, be ignorant of}}, from {{m|la|ne||not}} + {{m|la|sciō||to know}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|informal}} [[pleasant|Pleasant]], [[satisfactory]], [[complimentary]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montgomery Anne of Green Gables|passage="It's a lot '''nicer''' than going round by the road; that is so dusty and hot," said Diana practically, peeping into her dinner basket and mentally calculating if the three juicy, toothsome, raspberry tarts reposing there were divided among ten girls, how many bites each girl would have.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1998|artist=w:Baha Men|title=w:Who Let the Dogs Out?
|passage=When the party was '''nice''', the party was jumpin' (Hey, Yippie, Yi, Yo)}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rachel Cooke|quotee={{w|David Lodge (author)|David Lodge}}|title=Nice work|work=w:The Observer|date=2008-04-19|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2008/apr/20/fiction.davidlodge|issn=0029-7712|passage=What's difficult is when you think someone is saying something '''nice''' about you, but you're not quite sure.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210623031448/https://www.theguardian.com/books/2008/apr/20/fiction.davidlodge}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|informal}} Of a person: [[friendly]], [[attractive]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|You weren’t '''nice''' to me this morning.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2016-02-13|work=The Daily Journal|volume=83|issue=37|page=A11|author=Gene Lyons|quotee=anonymous|title=Understanding the 'Bernie Bros'|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/494323555/|text=Yes, please tell me how Shillary is the '''nicest''' corporate oligarchical servant, and how she will lovingly sell out the people who voted for her to her banker masters, with a twinkle in her fellating eye.}}
# [[respectable|Respectable]]; [[virtuous]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|What is a '''nice''' person like you doing in a place like this?}}
#* {{RQ:Hornby High Fidelity|page=14|passage=She was so '''nice''', in fact, that she wouldn't let me put my hand underneath or even on top of her bra, and so I finished with her, although obviously I didn't tell her why.}}
# {{lb|en|with ''[[and]]''|chiefly|informal}} {{n-g|Shows that the given adjective is desirable, or acts as a mild [[intensifier]]}}; [[pleasantly]], [[quite]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The soup is '''nice''' and hot.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=8|passage=We toted in the wood and got the fire going '''nice''' and comfortable. Lord James still set in one of the chairs and Applegate had cabbaged the other and was hugging the stove.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|informal}} Giving a favorable review or having a favorable impression.
#: {{ux|en|For ''Candy Crush Saga'', the critics were far '''nicer''' than the audience (7.9/10 vs. 3.1/10).|ref=[https://www.metacritic.com/game/candy-crush-saga/]}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|informal}} Showing [[refinement]] or [[delicacy]], [[proper]], [[seemly]]
#: {{ux|en|a '''nice''' way of putting it}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[silly|Silly]], [[ignorant]]; [[foolish]]. {{defdate|14th|17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=3|scene=1|passage=Old fashions please me best; I am not so '''nice'''<br>To change true rules for odd inventions.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} [[particular|Particular]] in one's conduct; [[scrupulous]], [[painstaking]]; [[choosy]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=2|book=II|passage=There is nothing he seemed to be more carefull of than of his honesty, and observe a kinde of decencie of his person, and orderly decorum in his habits, were it on foot or on horsebacke. He was exceeding '''nice''' in performing his word or promise.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=Mr Blifil, I am confident, understands himself better than to think of seeing my niece any more this morning, after what hath happened. Women are of a '''nice''' contexture; and our spirits, when disordered, are not to be recomposed in a moment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|translator=Joyce Crick|author=w:Sigmund Freud|title=w:The Interpretation of Dreams|location=Oxford|year_published=2008|page=83
|passage=But if I dispense with the dreams of neurotics, my main material, I cannot be too '''nice''' {{transterm|de|wählerisch}} in my dealings with the remainder.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Having [[particular]] tastes; [[fussy]], [[fastidious]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Joseph Bosworth; T. Northcote Toller|title=w:An Anglo-Saxon Dictionary|edition=2nd|location=Oxford|year=1898|entry=cís|url=https://www.google.co.nz/books/edition/An_Anglo_Saxon_Dictionary/rEy6MHD0R5EC|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|page=156|passage=''Choice, '''nice''' in eating''; fastidiosus in edendo.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[particular|Particular]] as regards [[rule]]s or qualities; [[strict]]. {{defdate|16th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma
|volume=II
|chapter=14
|passage=“Well, my dear,” he deliberately began, “considering we never saw her before, she seems a very pretty sort of young lady; and I dare say she was very much pleased with you. She speaks a little too quick. A little quickness of voice there is which rather hurts the ear. But I believe I am '''nice'''; I do not like strange voices; and nobody speaks like you and poor Miss Taylor. ..."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1818|author=w:Jane Austen|title=w:Persuasion|chapter=16
|passage="Good company requires only birth, education and manners, and with regard to education is not very '''nice'''. Birth and good manners are essential."}}
# [[show|Showing]] or [[require|requiring]] [[great]] [[precision]] or [[sensitive]] [[discern]]ment; [[subtle]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=w:Saki|title=s:Laura
|passage="It's her own funeral, you know," said Sir Lulworth; "it's a '''nice''' point in etiquette how far one ought to show respect to one's own mortal remains."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=Monsieur|page=131|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=1992
|passage=It would be a '''nice''' theological point to try and establish whether Ophis is Moslem or gnostic.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Clive James|title=North Face of Soho|page=242|publisher=Picador|year_published=2007
|passage=Why it should have attained such longevity is a '''nice''' question.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Easily injured; [[delicate]]; [[dainty]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[doubtful|Doubtful]], as to the outcome; [[risky]]. {{defdate|16th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|act=IV|scene=i|passage=[W]ere it good / To ſet the exact wealth of al our ſtates / Al at one caſt? to ſet ſo rich a maine / On the '''nice''' hazard of one doubtfull houre?|translation=Is it good / To bet all of our wealth / On one throw of the dice? To place so high a stake / On the '''risky''' hazard of one doubtful hour?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=28 Jul 1822|author=T. Creevey|journal=Reminiscences
|passage=It has been a damned '''nice''' thing - the nearest run thing you ever saw in your life.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Sometimes used [[sarcastically]] to mean the [[opposite]] or to [[connote]] [[excess]]:

* {{quote-journal|en|year=1710|author=w:Jonathan Swift|journal={{w|The Examiner (1710–1714)|The Examiner}} No. XIV
|passage=I have strictly observed this rule, and my imagination this minute represents before me a certain great man famous for this talent, to the constant practice of which he owes his twenty years’ reputation of the most skilful head in England, for the management of '''nice''' affairs.}}
* {{quote-av|en|year=1930|author=w:H.M. Walker|title=w:The Laurel-Hardy Murder Case
|passage=Here's another '''nice''' mess you've gotten us into.}}
* {{quote-song|en|year=1973|author=Cockerel Chorus|title=Nice One, Cyril!
|passage='''Nice''' one, Cyril!}}

